<Project>

  <!--
    THE CONFIGURATION MAPPING, FOR THE PROJECTS THE ROOT Directory.Build.props NEVER REACHED.

    Broiler.Input, Broiler.JS, Broiler.Media and Broiler.VM each carry a Directory.Build.props
    that deliberately does not chain to a parent, and MSBuild stops at the first one it finds
    walking up. The Directory.Build.targets lookup is separate, and three of those four have no
    such file — so THIS file does run for them, and importing the mapping here is what gives
    their projects the same answer about Debug-VM that every other project in the graph gets.

    Broiler.JS is the fourth and did not work that way: it is the one submodule with a
    Directory.Build.targets of its own, which stopped the lookup before it reached here and left
    its twenty-two projects compiling unoptimised under Release-VM. That file now chains to this
    one, which is a change inside that submodule; docs/vm-javascript-profile.md records it, and
    ci.yml asserts the outcome so it cannot regress quietly.

    Debug-VM/Release-VM are the first configurations whose project graph reaches into Broiler.VM,
    which is why the gap is worth closing now and cost nothing before: Release-Linux never built a
    Broiler.VM project, so nobody could observe it compiling unoptimised.

    The sentinel is set by the imported file itself. A project that already got the mapping from
    the props side skips this import; the imported file carries a second guard for the submodules
    whose own vendored copy already decomposed the configuration.
  -->
  <Import Project="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)eng\Broiler.Configurations.props"
          Condition="'$(BroilerConfigurationMappingApplied)' != 'true'" />

  <!--
    Making the names BEHAVE is above; making them EXIST is here, and the two cannot share an
    import. This one has to be unconditional and has to be last: a project that spells out its own
    <Configurations> in the csproj body overwrites anything the props side set, and this is the
    first point that sees the result. That file says what breaks when it runs any earlier.
  -->
  <Import Project="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)eng\Broiler.ConfigurationNames.targets" />

  <!--
    Standalone Broiler.HTML builds use the Graphics checkout nested inside that submodule.
    Root builds instead use the canonical top-level Graphics checkout, which for the Win32
    demo means the src/ layout Broiler.Graphics publishes today.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(MSBuildProjectFullPath)' == '$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)Broiler.HTML\Source\Broiler.HTML.Graphics.Win32.Demo\Broiler.HTML.Graphics.Win32.Demo.csproj'">
    <ProjectReference Remove="..\..\Broiler.Graphics\Broiler.Graphics.Windows\Broiler.Graphics.Direct2D.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)Broiler.Graphics\src\Broiler.Graphics.Windows\Broiler.Graphics.Direct2D.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Broiler.HTML reaches the top-level Broiler.Media checkout through the pre-src/ layout
    ("Broiler.Media\Broiler.Media.Image.Managed\"). That layout only ever existed inside the
    Broiler monorepo, which vendored Broiler.Media as a plain directory; the standalone
    Broiler-Platform/Broiler.Media repository has published its projects under src/ since its
    first commit, so the reference as written cannot resolve against the submodule.

    Delete this block once Broiler.HTML follows Broiler.Media into src/ and the gitlink here
    is bumped past that commit.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(MSBuildProjectFullPath)' == '$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)Broiler.HTML\Source\Broiler.HTML.Image\Broiler.HTML.Image.csproj'">
    <ProjectReference Remove="..\..\..\Broiler.Media\Broiler.Media.Image.Managed\Broiler.Media.Image.Managed.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)Broiler.Media\src\Broiler.Media.Image.Managed\Broiler.Media.Image.Managed.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
